Machine Real-Time Traffic Prediction Using Learning
Project Type: Final Year / Academic / Personal
Tech Stack: Python, Pandas, scikit-learn, Matplotlib, Flask (optional), Google Maps API (optional)

🧠 Objective:
To build a system that predicts the level of traffic (low, medium, high) in real-time using historical traffic data and machine learning models.

📊 Dataset Used:
Historical traffic speed and volume data (e.g., from city open data portals or Uber Movement).

Time-based features: hour, day of week, holidays.

Weather data (optional): rainfall, temperature.

GPS location or road segment ID.

🔧 Tools & Technologies:
Python (main language)

pandas, NumPy for data handling

scikit-learn, XGBoost for modeling

Matplotlib / Seaborn for visualization

Flask for optional web deployment

🛠 Workflow:
1. Data Preprocessing
Clean missing or noisy values.

Convert timestamps to features: hour, weekday/weekend, holiday.

Merge weather/traffic datasets.

Label encode or bin traffic speed into 3 classes: Low, Medium, High.

2. Feature Engineering
Features: hour of day, day of week, weather, road segment ID.

Target variable: traffic level.

3. Model Training
Tried Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost.

Used train-test split and GridSearchCV for tuning.

Best model: XGBoost, with 85–90% accuracy.

4. Model Evaluation
Used classification metrics: accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score.

Confusion matrix showed good performance in all 3 classes.

5. (Optional) Web Interface
Used Flask to create a simple web app:

User inputs time, location, weather.

App predicts traffic level.

🧪 Results:
Achieved ~87% accuracy in predicting traffic level.

Model predicts within 1 second — suitable for real-time use.

Visualized traffic patterns by time and location.

